  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nCold front will make a swift exit this morning with clearing \nexpected away from lake effect zones. Winds picking up out of the \nnorthwest with frontal passage, and this will keep low level \nmoisture streaming in, with depth increasing enough to warrant POPs \nafter 21Z as the inversion rises above 800mb. This lake effect will \nbe aided by a surface trough moving through in the 00Z Tuesday time \nframe. Precipitation amounts generally light. Rain along the \nlakeshore, but temperatures will drop below freezing for the late \nMonday night period, and some of the higher elevations areas of far \nNE OH and NW PA inland will see a light coating to a half inch of \nsnow by Tuesday morning. Highs in the 40s  all week. Tuesday night \nwill be the coldest night with widespread 20s away from the\nlake. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nNorthern stream upper level low will dive sharply into the \nMississippi Valley and phase with a southern stream trough moving \neastward from the southern plains region. Rapid cyclogenesis occurs \noff the Florida coast as a result, moving northward, and developing \nquickly into an occluded mid latitude wave cyclone. Large \nprecipitation shield to develop, encompassing the entire Ohio \nValley, moving into the southern Great Lakes. Expecting periodic \nrain Thanksgiving Day through Friday with this system, with low end \nPOPs lingering into the weekend. Pacific system in nature, so there \nwill not be any appreciable change in airmass in terms of \ntemperatures heading into next weekend.",
